// =====================================================================
// RULE:      Privileged role assigned at subscription or management group scope
// SEVERITY:  High
// ATT&CK:    Privilege Escalation / Persistence — T1098.003
//            (Account Manipulation: Additional Cloud Roles)
// CONTROLS:  NIST 800-53 AC-2, AC-6, AU-6 · NERC CIP-004-7 R4, R5 · CIP-007-6 R4
// SOURCE:    AzureActivity
// SCHEDULE:  run every 15m, look back 1h
// ENTITIES:  Account = Caller · IP = CallerIpAddress · AzureResource = _ResourceId
//
// WHY THIS RULE EXISTS
// Lab C narrows privilege by scope. A role assignment at subscription scope
// undoes that in one call, and unlike a credential it survives password resets,
// MFA enrolment and session revocation — which makes it the preferred
// persistence mechanism in cloud, not merely an escalation one.
//
// The discriminator that matters is SCOPE, not role name. Contributor on one
// resource group is routine. Reader at subscription scope is worth seeing.
// Owner at subscription scope is an event.
// =====================================================================

AzureActivity
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)
| where OperationNameValue has "MICROSOFT.AUTHORIZATION/ROLEASSIGNMENTS/WRITE"
| where ActivityStatusValue in ("Success", "Succeeded", "Accepted")
// scope depth: /subscriptions/<id> is 3 segments; anything deeper is narrower
| extend ScopeDepth = array_length(split(_ResourceId, "/"))
| extend AssignmentScope = iff(_ResourceId has "/resourceGroups/", "ResourceGroup", "Subscription or higher")
| where AssignmentScope == "Subscription or higher"
| project
    TimeGenerated,
    Caller,
    CallerIpAddress,
    AssignmentScope,
    _ResourceId,
    OperationNameValue,
    ActivityStatusValue
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// A LIMITATION TO STATE OUT LOUD
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// AzureActivity records that a role assignment was written, but the ROLE and
// the PRINCIPAL live in the request body, which this table does not reliably
// expose. Two honest options:
//
//   (a) Accept it: alert on the event, resolve the detail during triage with
//           az role assignment list --all --query "[?scope=='<scope>']"
//   (b) Enrich at source: send Entra ID audit logs (AuditLogs table) to the
//       workspace, where "Add member to role" carries both. That connector
//       costs ingestion — a real trade-off, and naming it is better than
//       writing a rule that silently cannot do what it claims.
//
// This lab takes option (a) and documents it. A detection with a stated blind
// spot is worth more than one with an unstated one.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// EXPECTED FALSE POSITIVES
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Your own Lab C work. Benign true positive.
// 2. Landing-zone deployments assigning roles to managed identities. Filter on
//    the deployment principal, not on the operation.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// COMPANION HUNT — standing privilege, as a point-in-time review
// Run monthly. This is the CIP-004-7 R4 artifact, not a detection.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// AzureActivity
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
// | where OperationNameValue has "MICROSOFT.AUTHORIZATION/ROLEASSIGNMENTS/"
// | summarize Assignments = count(),
//             Operations  = make_set(OperationNameValue),
//             LastSeen    = max(TimeGenerated)
//           by Caller
// | order by Assignments desc
